endpoint itself has a pole. Second, the paper connects to implicit regularization in least squares and high-dimensional regression. Gradient-flow and stochastic-gradient analyses compare optimization paths with explicit ℓ 2 \ell_{2} regularization (Ali et al. , 2019 , 2020 ; Zou et al. , 2021 ) ; the instance-wise GD-versus-ridge comparison of Wu et al. ( 2026 ) likewise stays within the contraction family. Our focus is the signed side, where the stable endpoint meets the MP pole barrier and the finite-time path becomes a mixed-sign implicit regularizer. Random-matrix approaches have also produced deterministic high-dimensional risk trajectories for stochastic-gradient dynamics in linear models (Paquette et al. , 2021 , 2025 ; Atanasov et al. , 2026 ) . Those results analyze stochastic optimization trajectories; our comparison concerns the admissibility wall for a negative-ridge endpoint and the finite-time full-batch path at the same signed shift. Third, our results build on benign overfitting and overparameterized linear regression. This literature characterizes how interpolation, ridge regularization, and spectral effective ranks determine prediction risk (Bartlett et al. , 2020 ; Hastie et al. , 2022 ; Dobriban and Wager, 2018 ; Tsigler and Bartlett, 2023 ; Nakkiran et al. , 2021 ; Liang and Rakhlin, 2020 ; Cheng and Montanari, 2024 ) . Source-condition analyses further clarify how the signal’s alignment with the covariance spectrum controls ridge and ridgeless behavior (Richards et al. , 2021 ) . Negative ridge has appeared as a response to implicit over-regularization: Dobriban and Wager ( 2018 ); Kobak et al. ( 2020 ) explain negative optimal ridge through high-dimensional asymptotics and low-variance covariates, Tsigler and Bartlett ( 2023 ) develop ridge theory in regimes where the optimal regularization can be negative, and Wu and Xu ( 2020 ) characterize signs of optimal weighted ℓ 2 \ell_{2} regularization in proportional limits. Those works characterize when the population-optimal ridge is negative and its sign. We instead isolate the endpoint–path asymmetry: the endpoint can take the useful sign, but not the floor-canceling magnitude , which lies beyond the sample Marchenko–Pastur pole and is reachable only along the finite-time path. Contributions. Our contributions are as follows. 1. Endpoint–path asymmetry and the Marchenko–Pastur pole barrier. Positive filters are contractions ( f ≤ 1 f\leq 1 ), so their only freedom is saturation or residual profile. The negative-ridge endpoint carries a pole of the useful sign but is admissible only below the smallest empirical eigenvalue; in the flat-tail benchmark the tail floor sits a bulk width a − μ ^ min + ≍ a ​ λ T a-\widehat{\mu}_{\min}^{+}\asymp\sqrt{a\lambda_{T}} above that edge, so the endpoint can take the useful sign but not the floor-canceling magnitude , which finite-time NS-GD reaches through its removable pole. 2. Constrained-prefix anti-shrinkage. In the exact design-conditional risk geometry of Section 2.2 , anti-shrinkage in a direction helps exactly when its missing-signal coupling exceeds the variance cost ( m i &gt; v i m_{i}&gt;v_{i} ), so the oracle asks for a selective correction. NS-GD realizes a leading-prefix-constrained form of this rule: by Corollaries 3 and 4 the displacement from ridgeless changes sign at a single spectral threshold—in strong-signal realizations the anti-shrunk prefix can be the whole observed spectrum—a data-adaptive prefix geometry unavailable to positive shrinkage and to the pole-limited endpoint. 3. Risk separations for common and heterogeneous heads, via a localized noncontractive proof. In the common-spike model the explicit continuous and discrete paths recover the spike with risk O ​ ( λ T / λ h ) O(\lambda_{T}/\lambda_{h}) , while admissible negative ridge pays Ω ​ ( λ T / λ h ) \Omega(\sqrt{\lambda_{T}/\lambda_{h}}) and nonnegative shrinkage retains a constant head-bias floor. The main random-design theorem replaces the common spike by the trace floor a = T k / n a=T_{k}/n and recovers distinct head scales simultaneously: the square mass S k S_{k} controls tail exposure, leakage, and the negative-endpoint lower envelope, and a weighted shape defect lower-bounds every uniform rescaling of ridgeless regression; flat and truncated power-law tails follow as corollaries. The proofs turn on a localized step: the shifted generator can be indefinite, so we align the realized clusters with an orthogonal invariant graph and apply Duhamel only inside the positive head block, whereby head–tail coupling must leave the head and return and enters quadratically—letting the Tsigler–Bartlett trace/effective-rank architecture accommodate the noncontractive signed path. 4. Deployable validation oracle and experiments. For the finite grid in Algorithm 1 , an independent hold-out sample selects a candidate whose realized prediction risk is within an explicit multiplicative factor of the best grid candidate, so every separation above transfers to the deployed estimator under an explicit validation-size condition. Paired simulations diagnose the predicted endpoint wall and the adaptive finite-time spectral shape. Roadmap. Section 2.2 presents the exact design-conditional oracle, Section 3 the two Gaussian head–tail regimes and their proofs, and Theorem 15 the transfer to the validation-selected estimator; experiments and discussion follow. 2 Row-space filters, risk, and finite-time geometry Let X ∈ ℝ n × p X\in\mathbb{R}^{n	imes p} be fixed for the spectral calculations, with Σ ^ = X ⊤ ​ X / n \widehat{\Sigma}=X^{	op}X/n and nonzero eigenpairs ( μ ^ i , v ^ i ) i = 1 r (\widehat{\mu}_{i},\widehat{v}_{i})_{i=1}^{r} . The response is y = X ​ β ⋆ + ε y=X eta^{\star}+\varepsilon , with 𝔼 ​ ( ε ∣ X ) = 0 \mathbb{E}(\varepsilon\mid X)=0 and Var ⁡ ( ε ∣ X ) = σ ε 2 ​ I n \operatorname{Var}(\varepsilon\mid X)=\sigma_{\varepsilon}^{2}I_{n} . Write b = X ⊤ ​ y / n b=X^{	op}y/n and θ i ⋆ = v ^ i ⊤ ​ β ⋆ 	heta_